<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client .NET</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-dotnet</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <!--
        Thin Maven wrapper over the .NET SDK. The commands below run ONLY under
        -Dpc.foreignClients - see the foreign-clients profile in the clients aggregator for why
        opt-in is load-bearing. An ordinary `bin/build.sh -am` therefore builds this pom and runs
        no .NET toolchain at all.

        The bare `build` and `test` arguments resolve because this directory holds exactly one
        solution file, Bz.Stub.ParallelConsumer.Proxy.Client.sln, which names both the library
        (src/) and its tests (tests/). Keep it that way: a second solution or a stray project file
        at this level makes every `dotnet` command here ambiguous, including the CI row's
        `dotnet format analyzers` verification, which also takes no project argument.

        THE TEST NEEDS THE PROXY MODULE'S TEST JAR, AND THE PROFILE BELOW IS HOW MAVEN ORDERS IT.
        `dotnet test` here spawns the conformance harness (Main, in
        parallel-consumer-proxy's test jar). An earlier note in this place said Maven could not
        order that for us and told the reader to build the proxy by hand first; it can, the same
        way the Go, Python and TypeScript modules already do it, and the clients matrix row - which
        cannot run a hand command - was red on exactly this. Locally the hand command still works
        and is still the fastest way:

            bin/build.sh -pl :parallel-consumer-proxy -am -DskipTests

        The test fails with that command in its message rather than skipping, because a test that
        quietly does not run is not a passing test. Recorded as a wave finding in
        docs/inflight/clients/dotnet.md (astubbs#242).
    -->
    <properties>
        <pc.foreign.skip>false</pc.foreign.skip>
        <pc.foreign.build.executable>bash</pc.foreign.build.executable>
        <pc.foreign.build.args>${pc.foreign.step} --tool dotnet -- dotnet build</pc.foreign.build.args>
        <pc.foreign.test.executable>bash</pc.foreign.test.executable>
        <pc.foreign.test.args>${pc.foreign.step} --tool dotnet -- dotnet test</pc.foreign.test.args>
    </properties>

    <profiles>
        <!--
            THE SIDECAR THIS MODULE'S HANDSHAKE TEST SPAWNS, AND WHY IT IS A DEPENDENCY.

            That test starts parallel-consumer-proxy's real Main as a child process and speaks the
            protocol to it, so `-am` has to build the proxy first - which it will not do for a module
            that declares no dependency on it. Declaring one here is what makes the CI matrix row's
            exact command work; the execution that writes the resolved classpath where the test reads
            it is managed ONCE in the clients aggregator, and this module opts in by declaring the
            plugin below.

            SCOPED TO THIS PROFILE DELIBERATELY. No client depends on the sidecar, and with the
            profile inactive - every ordinary build - there is no dependency edge here at all. The
            client obtains the protocol the way every non-JVM client does, by generating from the
            .proto by path with no Maven dependency; this edge is test-scoped harness plumbing in the
            opt-in lane and nothing this client ships.

            TEST SCOPE, THOUGH nothing here links against it - the sidecar is a PROCESS. The scope
            says WHEN the artifact is needed, which is only when the suite runs. The engine rung adds
            the proxy's TEST jar here too, for the harness that lives in it.
        -->
        <profile>
            <id>dotnet-sidecar-harness</id>
            <activation>
                <property>
                    <name>pc.foreignClients</name>
                </property>
            </activation>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
                    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy</artifactId>
                    <version>${project.version}</version>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
            <build>
                <plugins>
                    <!-- Opts this module into the aggregator's managed sidecar-classpath execution. -->
                    <plugin>
                        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                        <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
                    </plugin>
                </plugins>
            </build>
        </profile>
    </profiles>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <!--
                `mvn clean` MUST REMOVE WHAT `mvn compile -Dpc.foreignClients` BUILT, and for .NET
                that is not under target/. MSBuild writes bin/ (the assemblies) and obj/ (the
                intermediate output, which here also holds the protobuf and gRPC stubs Grpc.Tools
                generates on every build) beside each .csproj - three of them, src/ plus the two
                under tests/. Nothing else in the reactor would ever delete those, so without this
                block a clean tree still carries a full build of the conformance runner - Debug here,
                since `dotnet build` defaults to it and nothing in this module asks for Release.

                DELETED BY DIRECTORY PATTERN RATHER THAN BY PATH, to match this module's own
                .gitignore (`bin/`, `obj/`, `target/`) - that file is the module's existing
                statement of what is output, and a fourth project added to the solution would
                otherwise be missed here in silence.

                BOTH PATTERNS PER DIRECTORY ARE REQUIRED, and the shorter one alone is the trap:
                maven-clean-plugin removes a directory only once it is empty, so `**/bin` selects
                bin/ but nothing inside it and deletes NOTHING. Measured in a control before this
                was written (astubbs#242): `**/bin` alone left bin/Debug/net8.0/*.dll standing;
                adding `**/bin/**` removed the tree.

                WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: ~/.nuget/packages. The restored packages are this
                language's ~/.m2 - fetched dependencies, not build output - and `mvn clean` does not
                empty ~/.m2. `dotnet clean` is not used either: it needs the SDK present to delete
                files, and the whole premise of the foreign-clients profile is that the toolchain is
                absent on an ordinary machine. The clients aggregator's pom carries the full
                argument, including why no exec-driven clean can work at all.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-clean-plugin</artifactId>
                <!-- version from the root pom's pluginManagement -->
                <configuration>
                    <filesets>
                        <fileset>
                            <directory>${project.basedir}</directory>
                            <includes>
                                <include>**/bin</include>
                                <include>**/bin/**</include>
                                <include>**/obj</include>
                                <include>**/obj/**</include>
                                <include>**/TestResults</include>
                                <include>**/TestResults/**</include>
                            </includes>
                        </fileset>
                    </filesets>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
